n//   - Then multiply with x2 and stack with x3.\n//\n// To maximize performance, we parallelize over (batch, time, head).\n// Each thread handles one query token (one time step) for a given head.\n// It iterates over all keys, computing dot product and softmax.\n//\n// Since E = 512, num_heads=8 -> head_dim = 64.\n// Key/value matrices are [B*T, num_heads, head_dim].\n// For each query, we need to read all keys and values, leading to O(T) memory\n// reads per query. Shared memory can cache K^T and V for a block of queries.\n// But given typical sizes, a simpler approach may be sufficient.\n// We'll implement a straightforward kernel where each thread computes one\n// output element (after attention) but carefully coalesce reads.\n//\n// Let's break down into several kernels:\n// 1. Projection kernel: compute Q, K, V from x1, x2, x3 using in_proj_weight.\n//    We store Q, K, V in separate arrays of shape [B, num_heads, T, head_dim].\n// 2. Attention kernel: for each query token, compute attention and then\n//    multiply with x2 and stack with x3 directly to form the final output.\n//\n// Projection kernel: linear transformation y = x @ W^T + b.\n// We have x of shape [B*T, E], W of shape [3*E, E], b of shape [3*E].\n// Output shape [B*T, 3*E]. We can use one thread per output element.\n// Then we splice into Q, K, V.\n//\n// Attention + multiply + stack kernel:\n//   Each thread computes one element of the final output (after stack).\n//   The final output shape is [3, B, T, E] (before stacking dim permutation)\n//   and then we permute to actual stack_dim.\n//   But to avoid extra copy, we can compute the final layout directly.\n//   Let's define output as out[3][B][T][E] contiguous in memory in that order.\n//   Then after the kernel, we need to permute to stack_dim. We can either do a\n//   separate permutation kernel or compute directly into the final layout.\n//   Better: compute into final layout by calculating the correct index mapping.\n//\n// Let's first define the logical output before stacking as O[i][b][t][e],\n//   i in {0,1,2}, b in [0,B), t in [0,T), e in [0,E).\n//   O[0] = x3, O[1] = x1 * attn_out, O[2] = attn_out.\n//   The final tensor after torch.stack(dim=stack_dim) has shape [3, B, T, E]\n//   with dimension order: if stack_dim=0 -> [3, B, T, E] (already same),\n//   if stack_dim=1 -> [B, 3, T, E], if stack_dim=2 -> [B, T, 3, E],\n//   if stack_dim=3 -> [B, T, E, 3], if stack_dim=-1 -> same as 3.\n//   So the permutation can be handled by mapping output index to the above\n//   logical indices.\n//\n// For simplicity, we can compute the logical output in a contiguous buffer\n// and then apply a permutation kernel (or cudaMemcpy2D). But to save time,\n// we can fuse the permutation into the attention kernel by having each thread\n// write to the appropriate location in the final output tensor.\n//\n// However, the attention kernel naturally produces one value per element of\n// attn_out, and we also need x1*x2 and x3 copies. These can be written by\n// the same thread that computes attn_out for that element, but careful about\n// overlapping writes when stack_dim is not 0.\n//\n// We will proceed as follows:\n//   - Use a projection kernel to compute Q, K, V arrays.\n//   - Use an attention kernel that computes each element of attn_out\n//     and simultaneously writes three values to the final output tensor\n//     at the corresponding positions based on stack_dim.\n//     This avoids the need for a separate permutation and may improve\n//     locality.\n//   - But note: x3 must be copied as well. That can be done by a simple\n//     copy kernel or by same attention kernel: for each output element of\n//     x3 (O[0]), we can copy it independently. However, x3 copy does not\n//     depend on attention, so it could be overlapped. But for simplicity,\n//     we can combine everything in one kernel where each thread handles\n//     one (b,t,e) and computes:\n//        if stack_dim == 0: writes x3 at out[0][b][t][e]\n//        writes x1*x2 at out[?] depending on stack_dim,\n//        writes attn_out at out[?] depending on stack_dim.\n//     This requires each thread to compute attn_out for that (b,t,e), which\n//     means every thread for a given (b,t) would compute the same attention\n//     weighted sum redundantly for each e. That is wasteful.\n//   Instead, we want each thread to compute one query vector's attention\n//     (all head_dim elements) and then write them plus x1*x2 and x3 copies.\n//     So we can assign one thread per (b, t, head) to compute attention\n//     for that head and produce head_dim outputs. Then we can write those\n//     outputs, and also handle x1*x2 and x3 for those elements.\n//     But x1*x2 and x3 need to be written for every (b,t,e), and each\n//     thread handles a specific head's e range, so it can write those as well\n//     if we make sure each e is covered exactly once.\n//     This seems feasible: one thread per (b, t, head) computes attn_out\n//     for the e range corresponding to that head (head_dim contiguous in\n//     the last dimension if head representation is interleaved? Actually we\n//     will store Q,K,V in [B, T, num_heads, head_dim] and then after\n//     output projection, attn_out is in [B, T, E] with E = num_heads*head_dim.\n//     In PyTorch's MultiheadAttention, the output is formed by concatenating\n//     heads along the last dimension, then applying out_proj. So the mapping\n//     from head-specific output to E dimension is: for head h, positions\n//     h*head_dim to (h+1)*head_dim-1. So a thread for head h can write\n//     those e values, and also write x1*x2 and x3 for the same e values.\n//     Then all e are covered by some thread. So each thread will:\n//         1. Compute Q, K, V projections? Actually projection is separate.\n//         2. For the assigned (b, t, head), compute attention scores for all\n//            keys, apply mask, softmax, compute weighted sum of V (head_dim values),\n//            then apply out_proj to those head_dim values plus others? Wait,\n//            out_proj is a linear combination of all E elements, not just\n//            this head's output. So a single thread cannot compute the final\n//            attn_out element for a given e without all heads' outputs.\n//     So we need a two-stage approach: first compute attention outputs per head\n//     (shape [B, T, num_heads, head_dim]), then apply output projection (linear)\n//     to get [B, T, E]. The output projection is a matrix multiplication of\n//     [B*T, E] @ out_proj.weight^T + bias. That can be a separate kernel or\n//     done in the same kernel with cooperation.\n//     To avoid global synchronization, we can have a kernel that computes\n//     attention per head and writes to a temporary buffer (shape [B, T, num_heads, head_dim]),\n//     then another kernel that does output projection and also writes the final\n//     stacked result. This is simpler and may be efficient.\n//\n// So plan:\n//   Kernel 1: compute Q, K, V from x1, x2, x3 and in_proj_weight, in_proj_bias.\n//              Q = x1 @ Wq^T + bq, K = x2 @ Wk^T + bk, V = x3 @ Wv^T + bv.\n//              We can combine into one kernel that computes all three.\n//              Store Q, K, V in global memory as [B, T, num_heads, head_dim].\n//              To be contiguous, we can store as [B, num_heads, T, head_dim]\n//              or [B*T, num_heads, head_dim]. For attention, we want to access\n//              keys and values for all T for a given query. Coalescing and\n//              performance may be better with a different layout.\n//   Kernel 2: compute attention per head: for each (b, t, h), compute\n//              softmax(Q[b,t,h] · K[b, s, h] / sqrt(head_dim) + mask) over s,\n//              then weighted sum of V[b, s, h], producing attn_head[b,t,h,d].\n//   Kernel 3: output projection + stacking + multiply:\n//              For each (b,t,e), compute attn_out = sum_{h,d} attn_head[b,t,h,d] * out_proj.weight[e, h*head_dim+d] + bias[e].\n//              Then write to final output tensor:\n//                 O[0] (x3) at position corresponding to stack_dim\n//                 O[1] = x1 * attn_out\n//                 O[2] = attn_out\n//              This kernel also copies x3 as needed.\n//   This is three kernels, but we can fuse some if beneficial.\n//\n// To achieve high performance, we can implement Kernel 2 with shared memory\n// caching of K and V for a block of queries. Since head_dim=64, T=64, B=128,\n// each block can process multiple queries.\n//\n// Given the time constraint, we will implement straightforward but optimized kernels.\n// We'll ensure correctness and rely on memory coalescing and grid-stride loops.\n// The baseline is likely not extremely optimized; our custom kernel can beat it.\n\n// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Global sizes and constants\n// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n// Kernel 1: Projection.\n// Input: x (B*T, E), weight (3*E, E), bias (3*E), output Q,K,V.\n// We will launch one thread per output element of Q,K,V: total = B*T*3*E.\n// Each thread computes one element.\n// We'll organize: linearIndex = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x\n// total elements = B*T*3*E.\n// We map to (batch_time_idx, out_idx) where out_idx in [0,3E).\n// Then split out_idx into qkv (0:Q,1:K,2:V) and e in [0,E).\n// Then store into appropriate array.\n// Q, K, V arrays are each of size B*T*E, stored as float.\n// But to improve attention coalescing, we might store in layout [B, num_heads, T, head_dim].\n// We can perform transpose during projection: we store Q as [B, num_heads, T, head_dim]\n// where head_dim = E / num_heads, and the mapping from input dim e to (h,d) is h = e / head_dim, d = e % head_dim.\n// This layout groups data by head for faster access in attention.\n// So Q[b, h, t, d] = sum_{e_in} x1[b,t,e_in] * Wq[h*head_dim+d, e_in] + bq[h*head_dim+d].\n// Similarly for K and V.\n// So we need to compute Q[b,h,t,d].\n// We can have each thread compute one such element, so total threads = B*T*E (per Q, per K, per V).\n// We can launch three kernels or one kernel that does all three.\n// We'll do one kernel with 3*B*T*E threads, where each thread knows if it's Q/K/V.\n// Index mapping: global_id = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x\n// if global_id < B*T*E: compute Q, else if < 2*B*T*E: compute K, else compute V.\n// Or use 3D grid.\n// Simpler: three separate launches or one with block covering all; we'll use one kernel\n// with gridDim.x such that total threads = 3*B*T*E, each thread works on one element.\n// We'll use 1D thread index.\n\n__global__ void projection_kernel(\n    const float* __restrict__ x1, // [B,T,E]\n    const float* __restrict__ x2,\n    const float* __restrict__ x3,\n    const float* __restrict__ in_proj_weight, // [3E, E]\n    const float* __restrict__ in_proj_bias,   // [3E], may be null\n    float* __restrict__ Q, // [B, num_heads, T, head_dim]\n    float* __restrict__ K,\n    float* __restrict__ V,\n    int B, int T, int E, int num_heads,\n    bool has_bias)\n{\n    int total_threads = B * T * E * 3;\n    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n    if (idx >= total_threads) return;\n\n    int qkv = idx / (B * T * E);  // 0,1,2\n    int rem = idx - qkv * (B * T * E);\n    int b = rem / (T * E);\n    int rem2 = rem - b * (T * E);\n    int t = rem2 / E;\n    int e_in = rem2 - t * E;\n\n    // Map e_in to head and dim:\n    int head_dim = E / num_heads;\n    int h = e_in / head_dim;\n    int d = e_in - h * head_dim;\n\n    const float* x_ptr;\n    float* out_ptr;\n    if (qkv == 0) { x_ptr = x1; out_ptr = Q; }\n    else if (qkv == 1) { x_ptr = x2; out_ptr = K; }\n    else { x_ptr = x3; out_ptr = V; }\n\n    // weight row for this output: row = qkv*E + e_in (since weight is [3E, E])\n    int weight_row = qkv * E + e_in;\n    const float* weight_row_ptr = in_proj_weight + weight_row * E; // length E\n\n    float sum = 0.0f;\n    for (int i = 0; i < E; ++i) {\n        sum += x_ptr[b * T * E + t * E + i] * weight_row_ptr[i];\n    }\n    if (has_bias) {\n        sum += in_proj_bias[weight_row];\n    }\n\n    // Store into out_ptr at b, h, t, d layout: dimensions [B, num_heads, T, head_dim]\n    // index = ((b * num_heads + h) * T + t) * head_dim + d\n    int out_idx = ((b * num_heads + h) * T + t) * head_dim + d;\n    out_ptr[out_idx] = sum;\n}\n\n// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Kernel 2: Attention per head (no output projection).\n// Each thread computes one head output for one query: attn_head[b,t,h,d] for all d.\n// Actually we can have each thread compute all head_dim values for a given query and head.\n// That is efficient because we can load query vector once and iterate over keys.\n// We'll use one thread per (b, t, h). Total threads = B*T*num_heads.\n// Each thread will compute head_dim outputs, store to attn_head buffer.\n// We'll use shared memory to cache K and V for a block of queries.\n// But for simplicity, we can first implement without shared memory, then optimize.\n// Since T=64, each thread will read all T keys (each of head_dim floats) -> 64*64=4096 floats per thread.\n// That may be okay but can be improved.\n// We'll implement a shared-memory version: each block processes a tile of queries,\n// loads K and V for all T keys into shared memory for that head, then all queries in tile use it.\n// But heads are separate, so we need to organize blocks per head.\n// We can launch blocks over (b, h) and threads over t (queries). Each block loads full K and V for that (b,h)\n// into shared memory, then computes attention for all queries in the block.\n// Since T=64, block size can be 64 or 128 threads for queries.\n// We can have block of 64 threads (one warp per query? Actually each thread handles one query).\n// But then each thread computes all head_dim outputs sequentially.\n// Alternative: block of 256 threads: 4 queries per thread? Not needed.\n// We'll use one block per (batch, head), with blockDim.x = T (64) threads, each thread handles one query t.\n// Shared memory: load K and V for all T keys: size T * head_dim floats each.\n// head_dim=64, T=64 => 4096 floats = 16KB each, total 32KB. Should fit.\n// For larger T, may not fit, so we can implement tiling over keys in chunks.\n// But given the fixed model size (T=64), it fits. We can also add dynamic tiling for generality.\n// We'll implement a general tiling approach where we loop over key chunks.\n// The block will also load query vector for each thread from global memory into register (or shared).\n\n// Kernel 2: Attention per head, writes attn_head buffer.\n// attn_head layout: [B, num_heads, T, head_dim]\n// key_padding_mask: [B, T] bool, true means ignore (set score to -inf).\n// We use float mask_val = (mask ? -inf : 0).\n// The softmax denominator is sum of exp(score - max).\n// We compute scores = Q[b,h,t,:] dot K[b,h,s,:] / sqrt(d) + mask[b,s].\n// Then weighted sum of V[b,h,s,:].\n\n__global__ void attention_kernel(\n    const float* __restrict__ Q, // [B, num_heads, T, head_dim]\n    const float* __restrict__ K,\n    const float* __restrict__ V,\n    const bool* __restrict__ key_padding_mask, // [B, T]\n    float* __restrict__ attn_out, // [B, num_heads, T, head_dim]\n    int B, int T, int num_heads, int head_dim)\n{\n    int b = blockIdx.x; // batch\n    int h = blockIdx.y; // head\n    int t = threadIdx.x; // query position (0..T-1)\n    if (t >= T) return;\n    if (b >= B || h >= num_heads) return;\n\n    // Pointers for this batch and head\n    const float* Q_base = Q + ((b * num_heads + h) * T + t) * head_dim;\n    const float* K_base = K + (b * num_heads + h) * T * head_dim; // shape [T, head_dim]\n    const float* V_base = V + (b * num_heads + h) * T * head_dim;\n    float* out_base = attn_out + ((b * num_heads + h) * T + t) * head_dim;\n\n    // Load query vector into registers\n    float q[head_dim]; // dynamic size allowed in CUDA with constant? head_dim may be variable but we can use up to 64.\n    // Better to use local array with max size; we'll assume head_dim <= 128 and use a fixed array for speed.\n    // But for generality, we'll use a loop and recompute? We'll use a local array of size 64 (typical).\n    // Since head_dim = E/num_heads = 64 in this model, we can hardcode 64 for speed.\n    // But to be safe, we can use dynamic shared memory or just allocate local array with maximum.\n    // We'll create a template kernel with HEAD_DIM constant to allow loop unrolling.\n    // We'll instantiate for HEAD_DIM=64.\n}\n"
  }
}
